# Installed as /etc/tmpfiles.d/cathedral-sn39-validator-relay.conf.
#
# The third-party counterpart to cathedral-sn39-validator.tmpfiles. That file
# provisions the evidence PRODUCER's published tree under
# /var/lib/cathedral-public-evidence, including a logs/ directory owned by
# polaris. A relay host runs no producer and has no polaris account, so those
# lines describe a tree it never writes and name an identity it never creates:
# systemd-tmpfiles refuses a line whose user does not resolve. Shipping the
# producer contract to a third party therefore produced errors on every boot
# for directories nothing on that host uses.
#
# What a relay host actually needs is the pair of runtime directories the
# validator writes, created before the unit's first start. The unit's
# StateDirectory= and LogsDirectory= create them too, but systemd evaluates
# ConditionPathIsReadWrite= BEFORE it creates them, so on a fresh host the
# first start of an otherwise correct install is skipped -- and a skipped unit
# is reported as a successful `systemctl start`, not as a failure.
#
# Ownership and mode deliberately match what StateDirectory=/LogsDirectory=
# converge on for cathedral-validator-sn39-relay.service (User=
# cathedral-validator, Group=cathedral-validator-log, modes 0700 and 0750), so
# the two contracts cannot disagree about the same inode.
#
# Every mode and ownership field is ':'-prefixed, which systemd-tmpfiles
# applies ONLY when it creates the inode, for the reason given at length in
# cathedral-sn39-validator.tmpfiles: an unprefixed field is reapplied on every
# `systemd-tmpfiles --create`, including at boot, to a directory a running
# service already owns. Creation-only fields converge a fresh host on the
# reviewed layout and are a no-op on a host that already has one.
d /var/lib/cathedral-validator :0700 :cathedral-validator :cathedral-validator-log -
d /var/log/cathedral-validator :0750 :cathedral-validator :cathedral-validator-log -
